{
  "dom_id": "step-q20510",
  "name": "Question 8",
  "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/multivariable/14200695/assets/5e1d4dbd07dbf4e7f06b5ef2dd01ae2ef5fa82f3a5a78fb470388fad8b18af1e.png)\n\nThe graph of $y={f}^{′}(x),$ the derivative of $f(x),$ is shown above. Which of the following could be the graph of $y=f(x)?$",
  "worked_solution": "Recall that critical points of $y=f(x)$ occur when ${f}^{′}(x)=0$ or ${f}^{′}(x)$ does not exist. In our case, there is one critical point: \n\n$\\,$ $x=-1.5$ \n\nAlso, we have the following: \n\n- If ${f}^{′}(x)>0$ then $f(x)$ is increasing.\n\n- If ${f}^{′}(x)<0$ then $f(x)$ is decreasing.\n\nWe can now summarize the information from the graph in the table below.\n\n| $x$ | $(-\\infty ,-1.5)$ | $-1.5$ | $(-1.5,\\infty )$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| ${f}^{′}$ | $+$ | $0$ | $+$ |\n| $f$ | $↗$ | $\\text{no extr.}$ | $↗$ |\n\nLet's now sketch the graph of $y=f(x):$\n\n- First, we draw the critical point. We choose an arbitrary point on the vertical line $x=-1.5,$ as shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/multivariable/14200695/assets/8bee8a231cdfe8d41cf35fa50d97806d511a9477a8c92db98b1be11b70676928.png)\n\n- Next, we consider the behavior of $y=f(x)$ in the neighborhood of the critical points:\n\n- Since $f(x)$ is increasing $↗$ on $(-\\infty ,-1.5)$ and is increasing $↗$ on $(-1.5,+\\infty )$, the graph of $y=f(x)$ goes down to the left and up to the right of $x=-1.5.$\n\n- Furthermore, since ${f}^{′}(x)$ is decreasing to the left of $x=-1.5$, we must have ${f}^{″}(x)<0$, and, since it is increasing to the right of $x=-1.5$, we must have ${f}^{″}(x)>0.$ So, $x=-1.5$ is an inflection point at which the curve $y=f(x)$ changes from concave down to concave up.\n\nSo, we can draw as follows:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/multivariable/14200695/assets/89127ba4db33c1c2ca12ddac05eac1c7e5ebe6540519fda1b55600620c1369d7.png)\n\n- Finally, by connecting and extending the existing parts with a smooth curve, we obtain a possible graph of $y=f(x):$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/multivariable/14200695/assets/20784e268300c461c4567092230df10423b96d26287fde67074bab6690c1342f.png)\n\n**Note:** The graph we obtained is not the only possible one. Any similar graph that's shifted vertically up or down could also be considered a possible $y=f(x).$",
